;; A WebAssembly runtime, so an appliance can be extended without a shell.
;;
;; The property this buys is not packaging convenience. It is that "what may
;; this code do?" has an answer in the artifact rather than in a promise: a
;; component's imports are in its binary, and it cannot do what it did not
;; import. Combined with what Silt already knows about an image, that makes a
;; claim a security reviewer can check —
;;
;;   these 22 packages, these 74 kernel options, one component whose hash is
;;   X, which imports only sensor-read and mqtt-publish, on a system with no
;;   shell and no interpreter to add one.
;;
;; Today Silt owns half of that: the grants are flags in the unit file this
;; pack carries, and the solution hash covers them. Checking a component's
;; imports against what the profile permits is the next piece, and wants
;; evidence from running this first.

(pack wasm-runtime
  (version "0.1.0")
  (doc "Wasmtime, and a component run under explicit grants")

  (requires
    (buildroot "2025.02.16"))

  (external "br2-external")

  (provides
    (feature wasm-runtime)))
